WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:03.490
So how do we apply heat to food to cook it?

00:00:03.490 --> 00:00:07.030
We start with fuel, and we then take the fuel

00:00:07.030 --> 00:00:11.530
and either burn it or use it in some fashion in order to create heat.

00:00:11.530 --> 00:00:13.930
And that heat then transforms food from being

00:00:13.930 --> 00:00:18.410
uncooked and rather unappetizing to being cooked and rather delicious.

00:00:18.410 --> 00:00:22.300
So let&amp;#39;s start to think about the process of transferring heat

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:24.070
to food from fuel for a minute.

00:00:24.070 --> 00:00:29.110
So it turns out that wood or any fuel has what we call an energy density.

00:00:29.110 --> 00:00:32.409
This is an energy per unit mass-- usually the way

00:00:32.409 --> 00:00:37.420
that it&amp;#39;s reported-- of how much energy can be liberated by burning the fuel.

00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:42.590
So in the case of wood, the energy density is 14 megajoules per kilogram.

00:00:42.590 --> 00:00:45.520
That&amp;#39;s 14 million joules per kilogram.

00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:47.410
Joules is a unit of energy.

00:00:47.410 --> 00:00:49.360
It&amp;#39;s an SI unit of energy.

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:54.400
It&amp;#39;s measured in kilograms meters squared per second squared.

00:00:54.400 --> 00:01:00.470
And 14 megajoules per kilogram is the energy density of wood.

00:01:00.470 --> 00:01:03.170
Now it turns out that charcoal has actually a much higher energy

00:01:03.170 --> 00:01:03.670
than wood.

00:01:03.670 --> 00:01:06.790
The energy density of charcoal is about 30 megajoules per kilogram.

00:01:06.790 --> 00:01:09.260
So it&amp;#39;s almost a factor of two more, which

00:01:09.260 --> 00:01:11.510
for those of you who have cooked on piles of charcoal,

00:01:11.510 --> 00:01:14.070
this explains why you need less of it, really,

00:01:14.070 --> 00:01:16.180
to heat the same amount of food.

00:01:16.180 --> 00:01:19.750
So another way to cook food is not to burn fuel at all

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:22.300
but as to use an oven of some sort.

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:25.970
And there are at least two types of ovens.

00:01:25.970 --> 00:01:29.524
There are electrical ovens which have hot coils on them, which

00:01:29.524 --> 00:01:30.940
actually heat the air within them.

00:01:30.940 --> 00:01:34.420
And there are microwave ovens which send radiation in that interacts

00:01:34.420 --> 00:01:35.620
with and heats the food,

00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:38.500
If you go look at the specs on an oven, it

00:01:38.500 --> 00:01:43.590
turns out it doesn&amp;#39;t report the energy in terms of the fuel that is used.

00:01:43.590 --> 00:01:46.450
It actually reports the energy in what are called watts.

00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:51.350
Now watts as a unit of power-- the units of watts are joules per second.

00:01:51.350 --> 00:01:53.350
And the point of that, of course, is that if you

00:01:53.350 --> 00:01:57.660
put the food in the device for whatever period of time,

00:01:57.660 --> 00:02:01.754
then if you simply multiply the power output of the oven times the time

00:02:01.754 --> 00:02:03.670
that you put it in, that&amp;#39;s how much energy you

00:02:03.670 --> 00:02:08.169
could hope to absorb by the food, if it&amp;#39;s being done correctly.

00:02:08.169 --> 00:02:11.740
I mean, suppose you were to take food and put it in your oven for 10 minutes.

00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:16.000
So since every minute has 60 seconds, that&amp;#39;s 600 seconds.

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:19.870
If your oven has 1,000 watts, then that means

00:02:19.870 --> 00:02:23.410
that the total energy that you can possibly dump into the food

00:02:23.410 --> 00:02:30.610
is 1,000 watts times 600 seconds, which is 600,000 joules, which is actually

00:02:30.610 --> 00:02:35.560
less than what you would have in a kilogram of wood.

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:38.500
When you apply heat to food, what are you actually doing to it?

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:39.940
What is heat anyway?

00:02:39.940 --> 00:02:42.940
One definition is to say that heat is the total energy that

00:02:42.940 --> 00:02:47.650
results in the temperature of the system being whatever it is.

00:02:47.650 --> 00:02:50.960
Higher temperature, if you have a thing with higher temperature,

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:52.880
that means it has more heat.

00:02:52.880 --> 00:02:56.770
So at a molecular level, temperature is a measure

00:02:56.770 --> 00:02:59.560
of the motion of the molecules within a material.

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:02.380
So what&amp;#39;s actually happening is that the molecules in a material

00:03:02.380 --> 00:03:04.189
are jiggling around at some rate.

00:03:04.189 --> 00:03:05.980
I mean, you can just imagine them jiggling.

00:03:05.980 --> 00:03:08.380
And when you hear them up, they jiggle around faster.

00:03:08.380 --> 00:03:09.770
And that&amp;#39;s what temperature is.

00:03:09.770 --> 00:03:11.560
And it&amp;#39;s this jiggling that causes changes

00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:14.560
to occur in the material, which is at the heart of what we do when

00:03:14.560 --> 00:03:17.200
we cook and try to make food delicious.

00:03:17.200 --> 00:03:21.520
So a simple way to demonstrate this to you is to just show you a simulation.

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:23.740
So this is a simulation of a material.

00:03:23.740 --> 00:03:27.010
This is a simulation of water molecules essentially in a box.

00:03:27.010 --> 00:03:31.220
And initially the water molecules are at a rather low temperature.

00:03:31.220 --> 00:03:33.910
And so they&amp;#39;re sitting, barely moving at all.

00:03:33.910 --> 00:03:36.370
And now what we&amp;#39;re going to do is gradually raise

00:03:36.370 --> 00:03:38.470
the temperature in the simulation.

00:03:38.470 --> 00:03:40.630
And what you see is that when the temperatures is

00:03:40.630 --> 00:03:44.050
at a slightly higher level, the molecules start to jiggle a little bit.

00:03:44.050 --> 00:03:47.530
And they start to move and bump into each other a little bit.

00:03:47.530 --> 00:03:49.300
If we raise the temperature a little more

00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:51.636
the molecules start to move by each other.

00:03:51.636 --> 00:03:54.760
And when we raise the temperature a whole lot, the whole thing comes apart.

00:03:54.760 --> 00:03:57.370
And you see the molecules whizzing around everywhere.

00:03:57.370 --> 00:04:00.520
And that whizzing of the molecules, the jiggling and the whizzing,

00:04:00.520 --> 00:04:01.880
is the essence of temperature.

00:04:01.880 --> 00:04:06.630
And that is what we&amp;#39;re actually doing to a material when we dump in heat.

